WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.775
Hi, I'm James Murphy and this is mCoding.

00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:08.116
This video is about explicitly inheriting from object and whether or not you should do this.

00:00:08.116 --> 00:00:10.916
First off, have you ever seen this before?

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:15.448
Comment below and let me know because it's actually becoming increasingly less common.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:17.980
If you haven't seen it before, here's a few examples.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.655
This one is a Checker class from pycodestyle.

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:24.236
Here's an Iterator from the six library.

00:00:24.236 --> 00:00:30.685
Six is a compatibility library for code that's supposed to work in Python 2 and Python 3.

00:00:30.685 --> 00:00:37.001
And here's a base Immutable object in the very popular SQLAlchemy library.

00:00:37.001 --> 00:00:41.163
So, why did these classes explicitly inherit from object?

00:00:41.163 --> 00:00:44.233
Don't all classes inherit from object?

00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:47.728
Yes, in Python 3, all classes inherit from object

00:00:47.728 --> 00:00:54.324
whether or not you explicitly inherit from object, or use empty parens, or use no parens.

00:00:54.324 --> 00:00:59.580
With the exception of the names, A and B generate exactly the same bytecode.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.630
C technically generates different bytecode because you could do something horrible like this.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:08.030
Because Python is so dynamic, it actually allows you to do this.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.484
And in this case, C would inherit from int.

00:01:11.484 --> 00:01:17.364
But outside of that absolutely cursed behavior, A, B, and C do the exact same thing.

00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:21.281
In all of these cases, object ends up as a base class.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:24.721
The preferred way of writing this is the way that class A is written.

00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:28.539
It's the shortest and doesn't contain any redundant information.

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:32.235
So, why? Why do some people explicitly inherit from object?

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:34.440
Do they think it does something different?

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.369
Do they claim from the Python Zen that explicit is better than implicit and apply it to this case?

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:41.691
Well, actually no.

00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:47.375
The main reason you might see this is because in Python 2, these three were not actually equivalent.

00:01:47.375 --> 00:01:53.905
Way back in Python 2, there was a difference between so-called New-Style classes and Old-Style classes.

00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:58.745
If you didn't explicitly inherit from object, you would get an Old-Style class.

00:01:58.745 --> 00:02:03.104
This explicit inherit from object was introduced in Python 2.2.

00:02:03.104 --> 00:02:05.351
That was released in 2002.

00:02:05.351 --> 00:02:11.059
Way back then, types and classes were actually different concepts, which is no longer true in Python 3.

00:02:11.059 --> 00:02:15.119
And built-ins like int, float, and list didn't inherit from object.

00:02:15.119 --> 00:02:18.297
They were just their own built-in types written in C.

00:02:18.297 --> 00:02:20.783
Since they weren't classes, you couldn't inherit from them.

00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:25.161
And it was just really confusing with classes and types being different things.

00:02:25.161 --> 00:02:31.273
But Python 2.2 marked the start of the unification of the concepts of types and classes.

00:02:31.273 --> 00:02:37.812
That's where this base object was introduced and where all of the built-in types were changed to inherit from it.

00:02:37.812 --> 00:02:41.307
Of course, you don't want to break any old code or change what it does.

00:02:41.307 --> 00:02:47.195
So, that's why this explicit inherit from object was added instead of just changing the default behavior.

00:02:47.195 --> 00:02:52.818
Changing the default behavior to automatically inherit from object was eventually done in Python 3.

00:02:52.818 --> 00:02:57.653
That's one of the many backwards-incompatible changes that happened in the 2 to 3 transition.

00:02:57.653 --> 00:03:04.620
But before Python 3, you had to explicitly inherit from objects in order to make one of these so-called New-Style classes.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:11.001
New-Style classes included lots of new features that we take for granted today that Old-style classes didn't have.

00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:16.667
They added the descriptor protocol so you could use things like property, staticmethod, and classmethod.

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:20.316
They added support for dunder new and for metaclasses.

00:03:20.316 --> 00:03:26.101
And they introduced the Method Resolution Order or MRO, which allowed for things like super.

00:03:26.101 --> 00:03:30.120
New-Style classes were better than Old-Style classes in basically every way.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.062
So, that's why the best practice develop that you should just make all of your classes inherit from object.

00:03:35.062 --> 00:03:38.364
But Python 2 has reached its end of life.

00:03:38.364 --> 00:03:45.278
As of January 1st, 2020, even critical security vulnerabilities in Python 2 will no longer be fixed.

00:03:45.278 --> 00:03:49.768
Of course, the vast majority of us moved on to Python 3 many years ago.

00:03:49.768 --> 00:03:56.540
But this old quirk while completely unnecessary today still works and that's why you might see it in some older projects.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.167
It's just a relic from the past.

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:01.419
